.\"	@(#)fseek.3s	6.3 (Berkeley) 2/24/86
.\"
.TH FSEEK 3S  "August 1, 1992"
.ds ]W ANSI C
.SH NAME
fseek, ftell, rewind, fgetpos, fsetpos \- reposition a stream
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdio.h>
.PP
.B int fseek(FILE *stream, 
.B long int \fIoffset\fP, int \fIwhence\fP);
.PP
.B long int ftell(FILE *\fIstream\fP);
.PP
.B void rewind(FILE *\fIstream\fP);
.PP
.B int fgetpos(FILE *\fIstream\fP, fpos_t *\fIpos\fP);
.PP
.B int fsetpos(FILE *\fIstream\fP, const fpos_t *\fIpos\fP);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Fseek
sets the position of the next input or output
operation on the
.IR stream .
The new position is at the signed distance
.I offset
bytes
from the beginning, the current position, or the end of the file,
according as 
.I whence
has the value SEEK_SET, SEEK_CUR, or SEEK_END.
.I Fseek
undoes any effects of
.IR  ungetc (3S).
.PP
.I Ftell
returns the current value of the offset relative to the beginning
of the file associated with the named
.IR stream .
It is measured in bytes on UNIX;
on some other systems it is a magic cookie,
and the only foolproof way to obtain an 
.I offset
for
.IR fseek .
.PP
.I Fgetpos
stores the current value of the file position
indicator for the stream pointed to by
.I stream
in the object pointed to by
.IR pos .
The value stored contains unspecified information usable
by the
.I fsetpos
function for repositioning the stream to its
position at the time of the call to the 
.I fgetpos
function.
.PP
.I Fsetpos
sets the file position indicator for the stream pointed to by
.I stream
according to the value of the object pointed to by
.IR pos ,
which must be value returned by an earlier call to
.I fgetpos
on the same stream.
A successful call clears the end-of-file indicator for the
stream and undoes any effects of the
.I ungetc
function on the same stream. After call on an update stream,
the next operation may be either input or output.
.PP
.IR Rewind ( stream )
is equivalent to: \*(lq(void) fseek (\fIstream\fP, 0L, SEEK_CUR)\*(rq
.SH "RETURN VALUE"
.I Fseek
returns zero if successful, otherwise \-1.
.PP
.I Ftell
returns the current value of the file position indicator for
the stream, otherwise \-1L is returned.
.PP
Both 
.I fseek
and
.I ftell
stores an error code in \fIerrno\fR when unsuccessful. 
.SH ERRORS
The underlying functions of
.I fseek
and
.I ftell
are
.IR write (2)
and
.IR lseek (2).
The error conditions specified for
.IR write (2) 
and
.IR lseek (2)
apply to 
.I fseek
and
.I ftell.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
lseek(2),
write(2),
fopen(3S)
